window.MONSTER_CHUNK_109 = [{"name":"Giant Archerfish","cr":1,"xp":400,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"animal","subtypes":["aquatic"],"init":3,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":14,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":11,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex, +1 natural","hp":11,"hd":"2d8+2","hdvalue":2,"fort":4,"ref":6,"will":1,"speed":[{"type":"swim","speed":40}],"melee":"bite +4 (1d6)","ranged":"water spray +4 (1d3)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"water spray"}],"strength":11,"dexterity":17,"constitution":12,"intelligence":1,"wisdom":12,"charisma":2,"baseatk":1,"cmb":1,"cmd":14,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":6},{"skill":"Swim","value":11}],"environments":["temperate aquatic"],"organizations":["solitary","school (6-11)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This silver fish is 3 ft. long with vertical black stripes.","group":"Archerfish","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Water Spray","kind":"Ex","details":"By collecting water in its mouth, a giant archerfish can fire a powerful jet of water as a standard action. The attack has a range of 30 ft. with no range increment. If the giant archerfish scores a hit, the target is knocked prone."}],"description":"Giant archerfish are peaceful, surface-dwelling aquatic animals that shoot a powerful stream of water at land- or airborne creatures in an attempt to fell it into the giant archer's body of water where the fish quickly swims in and devours it. These creatures exist mainly on a diet of insects and small animals such as birds, shrimp, worms, and snails though some of the larger ones occasionally target small humanoids as prey. Giant archerfish are typically solitary creatures and rarely travel in schools. Schools of these fish, when encountered, don't work in concert with one another; every fish is on its own.\n\nGiant archerfish are three ft. long and have silver-colored bodies with vertical black stripes that begin just behind the head and fade as they near the tail. Their eyes are either cloudy blue or silver and its fins are slightly darker than its body. Some species of giant archerfish are yellowish gray with dark-colored eyes, but all have the vertical black stripes. Giant archerfish can grow to be five ft. long.\n\nThe giant archerfish spends most of its time near the surface where it uses its water spray to dislodge potential prey from their land-based perch and knock them into the water. The archerfish then darts in and bites. This fish's eyes are formed in such a way that allows it to see perfectly into the air or on land from a depth of six ft. without any distortion or refraction.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Giant Blister Beetle","cr":1,"xp":400,"alignment":"N","size":"Small","type":"vermin","init":0,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"tremorsense","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":16,"ac_flat_footed":11,"ac_mods":"+5 natural, +1 size","hp":11,"hd":"2d8+2","hdvalue":2,"fort":4,"ref":0,"will":0,"immunities":["mind-affecting effects","poison"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"bite +2 (1d6)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"blister spray"}],"strength":11,"dexterity":10,"constitution":12,"wisdom":10,"charisma":7,"baseatk":1,"cmb":0,"cmd":10,"cmd_special":"+8 vs. trip","skills":[{"skill":"Stealth","value":4,"special":"+8 in forests"}],"environments":["warm forests"],"organizations":["solitary","cluster (2-5)","click (4-9)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"A large beetle with a slick, dark green carapace approaches. The creature snaps its large serrated mandibles, black in color, together as it moves toward you.","group":"Beetle","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Blister Spray","kind":"Ex","details":"20-ft. line, 1d3 acid damage, Reflex DC 12 negates, usable every 4 rounds. Anyone damaged by the inky-black acidic fluid also develops painful blisters and welts, and suffers a -2 circumstance modifier on attack rolls and ability and skill checks for 10 rounds. A Heal check (DC 10) or any cure spell heals the blisters and negates the penalties. The save DC is Constitution-based."}],"description":"Blister beetles are nocturnal scavengers found in forests or underground.\n\nThey are non-aggressive creatures, but have voracious appetites and will, if food is scarce, attack just about anything that comes close to them. Their favored meal, however, is bees; both giant and normal. Their immunity to poison aids grants them protection from the deadly sting of giant bees (normal bees cannot penetrate their carapace). A cluster of beetles waits near an area frequented by bees and then spring to attack when a bee lands on a flower or plant. Another favored meal is grasshoppers. Blister beetles ambush these large insects and drag the prey back to their lair.\n\nBlister beetles reproduce and mature rapidly. The female deposits a group of 2d4 eggs in a hole in the nest. The male then sprays the eggs with its blister spray. Within three days the eggs burst open with an audible pop and out swarm the young blister beetles. Young reach maturity in about three weeks.\n\nWhen attacked, a blister beetle releases a foul stream of black oily liquid that irritates the skin of any living creature it hits. Afterwards, it moves to combat and strikes with its mandibles.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Giant Caribe","cr":1,"xp":400,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"animal","subtypes":["aquatic"],"init":2,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"keen scent"}],"ac":14,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":12,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +2 natural","hp":13,"hd":"3d8","hdvalue":3,"fort":3,"ref":5,"will":2,"speed":[{"type":"swim","speed":40}],"melee":"bite +4 (1d6+1)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"frenzy"}],"strength":12,"dexterity":15,"constitution":10,"intelligence":1,"wisdom":12,"charisma":2,"baseatk":2,"cmb":3,"cmd":15,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":10},{"skill":"Swim","value":10}],"environments":["any aquatic"],"organizations":["school (5-30)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This fish resembles a massive piranha with a jaw bearing rows of razorlike teeth.","group":"Caribe","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Frenzy","kind":"Ex","details":"A giant caribe that detects blood in the water goes into a killing frenzy, as do all other giant caribes within a 90-foot radius. Frenzied giant caribes attack until either they or their opponents are dead. A frenzied giant caribe can make one extra attack when making a full attack action. The attack is made using the creature's full base attack. Additionally, it gains a +1 bonus on attack rolls and a +1 dodge bonus to AC and Reflex saves. A frenzied giant caribe's swim speed increases by 10 ft. This frenzy lasts for 1 minute and can only be used once per encounter."},{"type":"Keen Scent","kind":"Ex","details":"A giant caribe can notice creatures by scent in a 90-foot radius underwater and can detect blood in the water at ranges up to 500 ft."}],"description":"The giant caribe is a rare form of giant saltwater piranha. They are pale bluish-green in color and about 6 ft. long, though larger specimens have been encountered.\n\nGiant caribes attack by swarming an opponent and biting with their razor-sharp teeth. Once blood is drawn, the entire pack goes into a frenzy, attacking twice each round.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Giant Fly","cr":1,"xp":400,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"vermin","init":2,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":14,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":12,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +2 natural","hp":16,"hd":"3d8+3","hdvalue":3,"fort":4,"ref":3,"will":1,"immunities":["mind-affecting effects"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"fly","speed":60,"maneuverability":"average"}],"melee":"bite +4 (1d6+3)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":14,"dexterity":15,"constitution":12,"wisdom":10,"charisma":9,"baseatk":2,"cmb":4,"cmd":16,"cmd_special":"+8 vs. trip","skills":[{"skill":"Fly","value":2},{"skill":"Perception","value":4}],"environments":["any"],"organizations":["solitary","swarm (6-11)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This man-sized insect has large, red, globular eyes, a body covered in hairy bristles, and two rapidly vibrating translucent wings.","group":"Fly","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","description":"Giant flies are larger relatives of normal flies. Like their lesser cousins, they are most often found in areas of garbage, litter, and refuse. A giant fly resembles a normal fly and can grow to a length of 12 ft., though most average about 6 ft. long.\n\nGiant flies attack by biting their opponents.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Giant Fly originally appeared in the First Edition Monster Manual II (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1983) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Giant Frog","cr":1,"xp":400,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"animal","init":1,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":12,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":11,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +1 natural","hp":15,"hd":"2d8+6","hdvalue":2,"fort":6,"ref":6,"will":-1,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"swim","speed":30}],"melee":"bite +3 (1d6+2 plus grab) or tongue +3 touch (grab)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft. (15 ft. with tongue)","special_attacks":[{"type":"pull","details":"tongue, 5 ft."},{"type":"swallow whole","details":"1d6+2 bludgeoning damage, AC 10, 1 hp"},{"type":"tongue"}],"strength":15,"dexterity":13,"constitution":16,"intelligence":1,"wisdom":8,"charisma":6,"baseatk":1,"cmb":3,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":14,"cmd_special":"+4 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Lightning Reflexes"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":9,"special":"+13 jumping"},{"skill":"Perception","value":3},{"skill":"Stealth","value":5},{"skill":"Swim","value":10}],"environments":["temperate aquatic","temperate marshes","warm aquatic","warm marshes"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","army (3-8)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature is a larger version of a frog. It has razor-sharp teeth lining its mouth.","group":"Frog","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Tongue","kind":"Ex","details":"A giant frog's tongue is a primary attack with reach equal to three times the frog's normal reach (15 ft. for a Medium giant frog). A giant frog's tongue deals no damage on a hit, but can be used to grab. A giant frog doesn't gain the grappled condition while using its tongue in this manner."}],"description":"Giant frogs leap into combat using their charge attack. Though giant frogs (the smallest variety) may flee or not attack opponents larger than themselves, the larger variety (dire, killer, and Abyssal) are ferocious and nearly mindless killing machines, attacking everything in sight that isn't a frog.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Giant Marmoset","cr":1,"xp":400,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"animal","init":2,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +3 natural","hp":16,"hd":"3d8+3","hdvalue":3,"fort":4,"ref":5,"will":1,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":40},{"type":"climb","speed":30}],"melee":"2 claws +4 (1d4), bite +4 (1d6) or 2 claws +4 (1d4),  tail slap -1 (1d6)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":10,"dexterity":15,"constitution":13,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":11,"charisma":10,"baseatk":2,"cmb":2,"cmd":14,"cmd_special":"+4 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":12},{"skill":"Perception","value":11},{"skill":"Stealth","value":6}],"environments":["warm forests"],"organizations":["pack (2-5)","family (6-11)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"The creature has bands of shaggy black and gray fur with a long tail and clawed paws.","group":"Marmoset","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","description":"Giant marmosets are larger, more aggressive relatives of the smaller marmoset. Their fur is gray or black, with black fur predominant over the back and neck. Their tails reach a length of 5 ft. and are colored in alternating bands of black and pale gray. They sustain themselves on a diet of tree saps and gums (using their sharp teeth to gnaw holes in the bark), small animals, and various fruits and nuts. Giant marmosets make their homes among the trees, rarely touching the ground unless hunting living prey. They are, on occasion, tamed by wild tribes of halflings and used as mounts in times of war.\n\nGiant marmosets prefer to attack from surprise, tossing large stones from the sides of cliffs at their prey, hanging from branches by their tail and swinging down to surprise their prey, or dropping from the trees on their unaware foes. They attack using their claws and bite or claws and a tail slap.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Giant Marmoset originally appeared in the Basic Edition module B3 Palace of the Silver Princess (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1981) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Giant Tick","cr":1,"xp":400,"alignment":"N","size":"Small","type":"vermin","init":0,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":17,"ac_mods":"+6 natural, +1 size","hp":9,"hd":"2d8","hdvalue":2,"fort":3,"ref":0,"will":0,"immunities":["vermin traits"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":10}],"melee":"bite +2 (1d4 plus attach and disease)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"blood drain"}],"strength":11,"dexterity":10,"constitution":11,"wisdom":10,"charisma":2,"baseatk":1,"cmb":0,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":10,"cmd_special":"+8 vs. trip","skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":4}],"environments":["temperate forests"],"organizations":["Cluster (3-6)","nest (7-12)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"Giant ticks appear as 3-foot long ticks. They are otherwise similar to normal ticks.","group":"Tick","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Blood Drain","kind":"Ex","details":"A giant tick drains blood, dealing 1d4 points of Constitution damage in any round when it begins its turn attached to a victim. Once it has dealt 8 points of Constitution damage, it detaches crawls off to digest the meal. If its victim dies before the giant tick's appetite has been sated, the giant tick detaches and seeks a new target."},{"type":"Disease","kind":"Ex","details":"Fifty percent of all giant ticks carry and deliver red ache with a bite attack. Roll individually for each giant tick in the cluster or nest. Red ache: Bite-injury; save Fort DC 11; onset 1d3 days; frequency 1 day; effect 1d6 Str damage; cure 2 consecutive saves. The save DC is Constitution-based."}],"description":"Giant ticks attack by dropping on their prey from above and stabbing with a hollow mouth tube. If subjected to fire or immersed in water, a giant tick detaches from its victim.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Giant Tick originally appeared in the First Edition Monster Manual (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1977) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Golden Cat","cr":1,"xp":400,"alignment":"N","size":"Small","type":"magical beast","init":2,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +2 natural, +1 size","hp":11,"hd":"2d10","hdvalue":2,"fort":3,"ref":5,"will":1,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20},{"type":"climb","speed":10}],"melee":"bite +5 (1d3-1), 2 claws +5 (1d2-1)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"luck/unluck"}],"strength":8,"dexterity":15,"constitution":10,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":12,"charisma":6,"baseatk":2,"cmb":0,"cmd":12,"feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":2,"special":"+10 balancing or jumping"},{"skill":"Climb","value":10},{"skill":"Perception","value":6},{"skill":"Stealth","value":10}],"languages":["Common (cannot speak)","telepathy 100 ft."],"environments":["any temperate"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This marvelous creature appears as a small housecat with sleek golden fur and glowing emerald eyes.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Luck/Unluck","kind":"Su","details":"When a golden cat selects an \"owner\" it grants that creature good fortune. Three times per day, so long as the golden cat is within 40 ft., the owner can reroll one die roll and take the most favorable result. Only one die can be rerolled each round. This ability is a power of the cat, not the owner, though the owner can simply telepathically communicate its desire to the cat. A golden cat cannot be forced to activate this ability if it doesn't wish to do so (unless magically compelled). Each time the golden cat activates this ability and the owner rerolls a die, all other creatures (including the owner's allies) within 40 ft. of the golden cat must reroll one die roll and take the least favorable result each time."}],"description":"These fickle creatures are highly sought after due to their magical ability to grant luck to one fortunate enough to be selected as an owner.\n\nSuch owners are known to spend extravagant amounts of wealth keeping the golden cat lavishly protected and comforted. Despite this, golden cats have been known to grow bored with an owner and strike out on their own in search of a new owner to bestow its good luck upon.\n\nWhile prized by their owners for the luck they bestow, golden cats are equally despised by the owner's allies for the bad luck they bring.\n\nGolden cats tend to lair in forested areas or hilly terrain, usually near civilized lands where the population is the greatest (it makes finding an owner easier). They favor humans and elves as owners above all other races, disdaining the less civilized races such as orcs, half-orcs, goblinoids, and so on. A golden cat never takes one of the aforementioned races as an owner.\n\nA golden cat appears as a normal cat with rich golden fur and green eyes. Like all cats, a golden cat enjoys catching mice, rabbits and small birds. They tend to avoid attacking things larger than themselves unless cornered and defending themselves. Even then, they seek escape as soon as possible.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Grimlock","cr":1,"xp":400,"alignment":"NE","size":"Medium","type":"monstrous humanoid","init":1,"senses":[{"type":"blindsight","modifier":"40 ft."},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +4 natural","hp":15,"hd":"2d10+4","hdvalue":2,"fort":2,"ref":4,"will":2,"immunities":["gaze attacks","illusions","visual effects"],"weaknesses":["blindness"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"battleaxe +4 (1d8+2) or 2 slams +4 (1d4+2)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":15,"dexterity":13,"constitution":14,"intelligence":10,"wisdom":8,"charisma":6,"baseatk":2,"cmb":4,"cmd":15,"feats":[{"feat":"Alertness","type":"bonus"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":7},{"skill":"Perception","value":9},{"skill":"Sense Motive","value":1},{"skill":"Stealth","value":6,"special":"+14 in stony environs"},{"skill":"Survival","value":4}],"languages":["Grimlock","Undercommon"],"environments":["any underground"],"organizations":["Gang (2-4)","hunting party (5-8)","pack (10-20)","tribe (10-60 plus 1 leader of 3rd-5th level per 10 adults)"],"treasure":"standard (battleaxe)","description_visual":"This hulking stone gray humanoid is dressed in rags and torn clothes, and grips a sharpened axe in its hands. Its hair is filthy and dark, and its eyes are clouded milky white.","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","description":"Grimlocks are evil and foul subterranean dwellers believed to be descendants of an ancient human race. Legends speak of long ago wars between various races that drove humans underground. For a while, they survived on what food they could forage, but eventually turned to cannibalism; beginning with small underground animals such as rats and other rodents, and eventually turning to aboveground raids on other races. Grimlocks dine on humanoid flesh and blood (with humans and dwarves being their favorite meals). They are primitive creatures, living in tribal communities of up to 60 or more individuals in underground caves and tunnels. Raiding and hunting bands often venture to the surface world to attack nearby settlements, capturing or killing those they encounter and returning to their lair to feast upon their spoils. Raids such as these are always conducted at night under the cover of darkness when grimlocks have the advantage. Grimlocks detest sunlight but are not harmed by it.\n\nWhen not raiding the surface world, grimlocks often battle with other subterranean races including drow, dwarves, duergar, and even other grimlock tribes. Such battles can consist of outright warfare, but most of the time the battles are simple raids into other underground lairs to procure food (usually human or dwarven slaves kept by the other underground races). When engaged in wars with other races, grimlock leaders often ride basilisks into battle. Some larger grimlock lairs often have at least one medusa in midst as well.\n\nA grimlock stands 5 to 6 ft. tall and weighs 150 to 200 pounds. Its skin is slate gray and its hair is oily and matted. The creature emanates a stench that most others find nauseating, yet to a grimlock, it's a means of identification, for each scent is unique to a grimlock. Such fine distinctions are noticeable to other grimlocks, and possibly other creatures with a strong olfactory sense.\n\nDue to their lack of sight, grimlocks prefer melee to ranged combat and close on enemies quickly when engaged. They attack with their menacing axes or powerful slams, slashing or pummeling their foes until their opponents are dead. Opponents that attempt to flee are run down and killed. Grimlocks that fall in combat are \"honored\" by being carried off the field of battle and devoured by their own.\n\nRecent forays into underground caverns and caves by an intrepid band of adventurers speak of another race of grimlocks, civilized, and non-cannibalistic. These same adventurers speak of a large underground city full of these creatures. Whether these are truly advanced grimlocks or another race entirely is yet to be confirmed.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Gryph","cr":1,"xp":400,"alignment":"NE","size":"Small","type":"magical beast","init":2,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":13,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":11,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +1 size, +0 natural","hp":15,"hd":"2d10+4","hdvalue":2,"fort":5,"ref":5,"will":1,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"fly","speed":50,"maneuverability":"good"}],"melee":"bite +5 (1d6/x3), legs +5 (attach)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"implant eggs"}],"strength":11,"dexterity":15,"constitution":14,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":12,"charisma":7,"baseatk":2,"cmb":1,"cmd":13,"cmd_special":"+6, +8, +10 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Fly","value":8},{"skill":"Perception","value":5},{"skill":"Stealth","value":10}],"environments":["any underground"],"organizations":["flock (2-7 plus 1 female per 3 gryphs)","throng (8-15 plus 1 female per 3 gryphs)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This small jet-black avian is about the size of an eagle. It has multiple legs and a needle-like beak.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Attach","kind":"Ex","details":"When a gryph hits with its legs, its talons latch tightly onto the target, anchoring it in place. An attached gryph is effectively grappling its prey. The gryph loses its Dexterity bonus to AC and has an AC of 11, but holds on with great tenacity and gains a +4 bonus on attack rolls with its bite. Alternatively, an attached gryph can attempt to implant eggs in the target. An attached gryph has a +8 racial bonus to maintain its grapple on a foe once it is attached. An attached gryph can be struck with a weapon or grappled itself-if its prey manages to win a grapple check or Escape Artist check against it, the gryph is removed."},{"type":"Implant Eggs","kind":"Ex","details":"Once per day, a gryph can implant eggs into a helpless target or a target that it is currently attached to as a full-round action by extending an ovipositor from its abdomen and penetrating the victim's flesh by making a successful +4 melee attack. On a hit, the ovipositor inflicts 1 damage and implants 1d4 eggs into the victim's body. As long as a victim has gryph eggs implanted in his body, he is sickened as the eggs draw nutrients from his blood and flesh. The eggs grow swiftly, hatching in a mere 1d4 minutes into ravenous baby gryphs that immediately burrow out of the victim's body. This inflicts 2 points of Constitution damage per baby gryph, after which the hatchlings immediately take wing and fly away. Treat a gryph hatchling as a bat if statistics are needed. Gryph eggs can be cut out of a victim's body with a successful DC 20 Heal check made as a full-round action that inflicts 1 point of damage per attempt. Although immunity to disease offers no special protection against gryph egg implantation, remove disease or heal immediately destroys any implanted gryph eggs."}],"description":"Gryphs are bird-like creatures found in dungeons, ruins, and caverns.\n\nThey are typically scavengers, feeding on dead rodents and other small animals, but in times when food is scarce they take on a more predatory role and become hunters, craving the flesh and blood of warm-blooded creatures.\n\nA gryph has four, six, or eight legs. Its feathers are jet black, as are its talons and beak, and its eyes are reddish-brown in color. The gryph has a long, needle-like beak that it uses with deadly accuracy.\n\nA gryph attacks by swooping down at its prey at high speed and stabbing with its beak. If a female gryph is present, she attempts to grapple an opponent and implant her eggs in the prey.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Gryph originally appeared in the First Edition Fiend Folio (© TSR/ Wizards of the Coast, 1981) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Half-ogre","cr":1,"xp":400,"alignment":"CE","size":"Medium","type":"humanoid","subtypes":["giant"],"init":0,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":10,"ac_flat_footed":15,"ac_mods":"+3 armor, +2 natural","hp":13,"hd":"2d8+4","hdvalue":2,"fort":5,"ref":0,"will":0,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20,"special":"30 ft. without armor"}],"melee":"greatsword +5 (2d6+4/19-20), longspear +4 (1d8+4/x3)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft. (10 ft. with longspear)","strength":17,"dexterity":10,"constitution":14,"intelligence":9,"wisdom":10,"charisma":9,"baseatk":1,"cmb":4,"cmd":14,"feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Focus (greatsword)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":8}],"languages":["Common"],"sq":[{"type":"ogre blood"}],"environments":["temperate hills","temperate mountains"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","gang (2-4)","band (5-8)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This being resembles a somewhat ugly human with dark toned skin and matted dark hair. It wears tattered skins over a suit of hide armor.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Ogre Blood","kind":"Ex","details":"For all special abilities and effects, a half-ogre is considered an ogre."}],"description":"Half-ogres are rare crossbreeds of human and ogre. Standing a few ft. shorter than their ogre kin and a few ft. taller than their human kin, half-ogres have strength as well as speed and intelligence (relative to other ogres) in their favor. Their skin and hair color generally match that of their ogre parent, with dark tones such as gray, brown, or olive being the most prevalent. Half-ogres speak Common. Those with an Intelligence score of at least 10 also speak Giant. A half-ogre's usual weapon of choice is a greatsword or a longspear. They sometimes employ longswords and large wooden shields. Half-Ogre Society Half-ogres, though generally outcasts among humans and feared for their ugliness and size, can find some acceptance among ogres. Half-ogres in an ogre band need to prove themselves constantly to their larger kin, however. For this reason, half-ogres found among an ogre band are cruel, violent, and strong; weaker half-ogres usually wind up in the stew pot. Most half-ogres found among full-blooded ogres are leaders of the ogre band or are at least well on their way to becoming leaders. Their long years suffering the harsh treatment of their kin help half-ogres develop a sense of cunning and a strong will to survive. Therefore, ogres under the leadership of a half-ogre fight more effectively, even engaging in planned ambushes and complicated tactics that are beyond most ogres. On rare occasions, half-ogres collect into hybrid communities of other half-ogre races (such as orogs and ogrillons) or humanoids (such as orcs and half-orcs). These rogue bands of outcasts form bandit clans or marauding groups that are the bane of other humanoid communities in their area.","as_character":[{"description":"Half-ogres possess racial Hit Dice and may have class levels. Halfogres have the following racial traits.","racial_traits":[{"type":"Ability Score Bonuses","value":"+6 Strength, +4 Constitution, -2 Intelligence, -2 Charisma. Halfogres are fierce warriors, but weak minded."},{"type":"Darkvision","value":"Half-ogres can see in the dark up to 60 ft."},{"type":"Racial Hit Dice","value":"A half-ogre begins with two levels of humanoid, which provide 2d8 Hit Dice, a base attack bonus of +1, and base saving throw bonuses of Fort +3, Ref +0, and Will +0."},{"type":"Racial Skills","value":"A half-ogre's humanoid levels give it skill points equal to 2 x (2 + Int modifier)."},{"type":"Racial Feats","value":"A half-ogre's humanoid levels give it one feat."},{"type":"AC","value":"+2 natural armor bonus."},{"type":"Weapon proficiency","value":"A half-ogre is proficient with the greatsword and all simple weapons."},{"type":"Special Qualities","value":"Ogre blood."},{"type":"Languages","value":"Half-ogres begin play speaking Common, and can also speak giant if they have an Intelligence of at least 10. Half-ogres with high Intelligence scores can choose from the following languages: Orc, Goblin, Troll."}]}],"ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Half-Ogre originally appeared in Dragon #29 (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1979) and is used by permission.","gear":"hide armor, greatsword, longspear"},{"name":"Rhianna Horse","cr":1,"xp":400,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"animal","init":1,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":14,"ac_touch":10,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +4 natural, -1 size","hp":16,"hd":"3d8+3","hdvalue":3,"fort":6,"ref":4,"will":2,"weaknesses":["encumbrance penalty"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":60}],"melee":"2 hooves -1 (1d4+1)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":16,"dexterity":13,"constitution":17,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":13,"charisma":6,"baseatk":2,"cmb":6,"cmd":17,"cmd_special":"+4 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Endurance"},{"feat":"Nimble Moves"},{"feat":"Run","type":"bonus"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":5,"special":"+9 to balance"},{"skill":"Perception","value":6}],"sq":[{"type":"dexterous"},{"type":"docile"},{"type":"hardy"}],"environments":["temperate plains"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","herd (3-30)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This horse is shorter and less elegantly muscled than most other riding breeds. It has a shaggy brown coat and a long, thick mane.","group":"Horse","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Dexterous","kind":"Ex","details":"Rhiannas convey a +2 bonus to their riders' Ride checks to avoid obstacles."},{"type":"Docile","kind":"Ex","details":"Unless specifically trained for combat (see the Handle Animal skill), a Rhianna's hooves are treated as secondary attacks."},{"type":"Encumbrance Penalty","kind":"Ex","details":"Rhiannas lose the benefits of the Run feat whenever they are encumbered. They may still run at 4 times their speed with light encumbrance (up to 228 lbs.), and 3 times their speed with medium (459 lbs.) or heavy (690 lbs.) encumbrance."},{"type":"Hardy","kind":"Ex","details":"Rhiannas are extremely durable creatures. Unlike with other mounts, anytime a hustle, forced march or spurred movement would do damage to these horses, they are allowed a Constitution save to negate the damage."}],"description":"Domesticated Rhiannas retain the notable traits of their wild ancestors: small size, endurance and sure-footedness. They are especially popular with woodsmen - those who opt for mounted travels - due to their hardiness, dexterity an companionship. Most homesteaders choose the Rhianna breed as well, as they are equally suited to long-distance rides over varied terrain and drawing a market cart over rutted roads. Only the heaviest farm labors, such as breaking sod, spring plowing and pulling stumps, are reserved for larger draft horses, and many of the farmers of the valley make do with Rhiannas alone.\n\nRhianna horses differ from other light warhorses in several ways. Although they are similar in build to warhorses, they cannot fight with a rider. While their speed is the same, they don't perform as well when heavily loaded. Due to their evolution in a valley littered with obstacles such as trees, streams, boulders and scrub growth, they are extremely agile and adept at negotiating obstacles. They are resistant to damage incurred by fatigue, and may be pushed harder and for longer periods of time without the risk of injury. Because of their intolerance for encumbrance, Rhiannas make ideal mounts for lightly armed and armored heroes. Even encumbered, their ability to increase their speed for short distances gives them an advantage over common riding horses. This is especially useful when difficult terrain disallows running and charging.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"Original author Nathan Douglas Paul  Originally appearing in The Eamonvale Incursion (© Necromancer Games, 2008)"},{"name":"Huggermugger","cr":1,"xp":400,"alignment":"CN","size":"Small","type":"humanoid","subtypes":["dark folk"],"init":3,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"auras":["confusion aura"],"ac":15,"ac_touch":14,"ac_flat_footed":15,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex, +1 natural, +1 size | natural cunning","hp":9,"hd":"2d8","hdvalue":2,"fort":0,"ref":6,"will":1,"immunities":["natural cunning"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"hook-blade +5 (1d4))","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":11,"dexterity":16,"constitution":10,"intelligence":11,"wisdom":13,"charisma":12,"baseatk":1,"cmb":0,"cmd":13,"feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":6},{"skill":"Sleight of Hand","value":11},{"skill":"Stealth","value":15}],"languages":["chatter (see description below)"],"environments":["any underground"],"organizations":["band (4-9)","swarm (10-20)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This small humanoid stands no ore than 3 to 4-ft. tall, with cropped, black hair, hidden beneath a black hat, pulled low so as to hide its facial features. It conceals the rest of its body beneath a long dark robe.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Confusion Aura","kind":"Su","details":"A group of three or more huggermuggers can, as a free action, create an aura of confusion. Anyone within 40 ft. of the huggermuggers must succeed at a Will save (DC 12, +1 per huggermugger in the group) or be affected as by a confusion spell for 5 rounds. All huggermuggers in the group must be within 30 ft. of each other to create this aura. Each huggermugger may contribute to the confusion aura once per day. A creature that makes a successful save cannot be affected by the confusion aura of any huggermuggers in that group for one day. The save DC is Charisma-based."},{"type":"Natural Cunning","kind":"Ex","details":"Huggermuggers possess innate cunning and logical ability. This makes them immune to maze spells, prevents them from ever becoming lost, and enables them to track enemies. Further, they are never caught flat-footed."}],"description":"Huggermuggers are small, underground dwelling humanoids that relish in chaos, disorder, trickery, and the fine art of thieving. These degenerate humanoid creatures have only been encountered in large cities with extensive sewer systems, but it isn't known how their race has spread or how it developed. Most compare them to rats for swarming up from the filth to feed on human society like great parasites. A few fear-mongering city dwellers loudly speculate that the huggermuggers maintain vast tunnel complexes that connect all of the large cities of the world, and that some day the vile race will issue up from the depths as an invading army. Huggermuggers don't speak, other than the incessant chattering and mumbling they seem to constantly indulge in. Their language has yet to be translated by sages, so what they call themselves remains a mystery. The name \"huggermugger\" is a nonsense word akin to \"boogeyman,\" being a name used in stories to scare children into remaining close to home and not exploring the sewers and dark alleys of the city. Curiously, their skin is pale and cold to the touch, regardless of the actual temperature in the surrounding area. A band of huggermuggers slowly circles its opponents, chattering and mumbling so as to cause confusion. Each round they circle a foe, one huggermugger moves in, attacks with its hook-blade, and then falls back into the circle as another prepares to attack. If outnumbered or outclassed, huggermuggers prefer to attack from ambush, confusing their opponents before robbing or slaying them.\n\nHook-Blade (Exotic Light Melee Weapon) The hook-blade is 13 inches long with a wooden handle and finely sharpened, curved blade on one end. A smaller blade protrudes from the other side of the weapon. It resembles a kama with a smaller curved blade opposite the main blade. Hook-Blade: Cost 7 gp; Damage 1d4 (S), 1d6 (M); Crit x2; Weight 2 lb.; Type slashing; Special disarm.","as_character":[{"description":"","racial_traits":[{"type":"Ability Score Bonuses","value":"Dex +6, Wis +2, Cha +2. Huggermugger leaders tend to be rogues or multiclass rogues (rogue/sorcerer most often). Huggermugger clerics can choose from two of the following domains: Chaos, Travel, Protection, and Trickery."},{"type":"Darkvision","value":"huggermuggers can see in the dark up to 60 ft."},{"type":"Small size","value":"huggermuggers gain a +1 bonus to AC, +1 bonus to attack rolls, +4 bonus on Hide checks, -4 penalty on grapple checks, lifting and carrying limits one-half those of Medium characters."},{"type":"Low-light vision","value":"a huggermugger can see twice as far as a human under low-light conditions."},{"type":"Racial Hit Dice","value":"A huggermugger begins with two levels of humanoid, which provide 2d8 Hit Dice, a base attack bonus of +1, and base saving throw bonuses of Fort +3, Ref +0, and Will +0."},{"type":"Racial Skills","value":"A huggermugger's humanoid levels give it skill points equal to 2 x (2 + Int modifier). They gain a +4 racial bonus to Sleight of Hand, and Stealth checks."},{"type":"Racial Feats","value":"A huggermugger's humanoid levels give it one feat."},{"type":"AC","value":"+1 natural armor bonus."},{"type":"Racial Weapon Proficiency","value":"A huggermugger doesn't require an exotic weapon proficiency to wield a hook-blade."},{"type":"Special Attacks","value":"Confusion Aura."},{"type":"Special Qualities","value":"Natural Cunning."},{"type":"Languages","value":"Huggermuggers PCs begin play speaking their own gibberish as well as a smattering of Common provided they have an Intelligence of at least 10. Huggermuggers with high Intelligence scores can choose from the following languages: Aklo, Dark Folk, Dwarven, Goblin, Orc, and Undercommon,."}]}],"ecology":"","habitat_society":"","gear":"hook-blade"},{"name":"Killer Frog","cr":1,"xp":400,"alignment":"N","size":"Small","type":"animal","init":1,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":14,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +2 natural, +1 size","hp":6,"hd":"1d8+2","hdvalue":1,"fort":4,"ref":3,"will":-1,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":10},{"type":"swim","speed":30}],"melee":"2 claws +2 (1d4+1 plus grab), bite +2 (1d4+1)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"rake","details":"2 claws +2, 1d4+1"}],"strength":12,"dexterity":13,"constitution":14,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":9,"charisma":6,"baseatk":0,"cmb":0,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":11,"cmd_special":"+4 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Improved Natural Attack (claw)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":5,"special":"+9 jumping"},{"skill":"Perception","value":3},{"skill":"Stealth","value":9},{"skill":"Swim","value":12}],"environments":["any aquatic","temperate land","any underground","warm land"],"organizations":["pack (2-5)","cluster (4-7)","swarm (3-18)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This frog stands partially erect and has dark green skin fading to light on its underbelly.","group":"Frog","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","description":"Killer frogs are similar to their dire cousins, except that they stand partially erect and use their front claws as well as their bite. Killer frogs are created by an evil mutation of dire frogs through a practice thought to be known only to the worshipers of Tsathogga. Killer frogs, being more humanoid in appearance, don't have adhesive tongues.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Land Lamprey","cr":1,"xp":400,"alignment":"N","size":"Small","type":"magical beast","init":2,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +3 natural, +1 size","hp":5,"hd":"1d10","hdvalue":1,"fort":2,"ref":4,"will":1,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":40}],"melee":"bite +3 (1d3-1 plus attach)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"attach"},{"type":"blood drain","details":"1d4 Constitution"}],"strength":8,"dexterity":14,"constitution":11,"intelligence":1,"wisdom":12,"charisma":2,"baseatk":0,"cmb":-2,"cmb_special":"+8 vs. grapple","cmd":10,"feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":5},{"skill":"Stealth","value":6}],"environments":["temperate forests","temperate plains","any underground"],"organizations":["solitary","pack (2-5)","swarm (6-11)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature is a 3-foot long blackish-green eel with a large downward facing mouth lined with sharpened teeth.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Blood Drain","kind":"Ex","details":"A land lamprey drains blood, dealing 1d4 points of Constitution damage in any round when it begins its turn attached to a victim. Once it has dealt 4 points of Constitution damage, it detaches and slithers off to digest the meal. If its victim dies before the land lamprey's appetite has been sated, the land lamprey detaches and seeks a new target."}],"description":"Land lampreys can be found in all but the hottest and the coldest environments. They prefer the dark and dampness of the subterranean world and so are most often encountered there. A land lamprey attacks by attaching to a victim and draining blood. An application of fire causes the land lamprey to releases its hold and move away from the source of the flame.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Land Lamprey originally appeared in the First Edition Monster Manual II (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1983) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Larval Slime Crawler","cr":1,"xp":400,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"aberration","init":2,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +3 natural","hp":5,"hd":"1d8+1","hdvalue":1,"fort":1,"ref":2,"will":3,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20},{"type":"climb","speed":10}],"melee":"tentacles +1 (1d3+1 plus grab) and bite +1 (1d4+1)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"constrict","details":"1d3+1"}],"strength":12,"dexterity":14,"constitution":12,"intelligence":1,"wisdom":12,"charisma":6,"baseatk":0,"cmb":1,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":13,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":9},{"skill":"Escape Artist","value":8},{"skill":"Perception","value":8}],"sq":[{"type":"slippery"}],"environments":["any land","any underground"],"organizations":["solitary","cluster (2-5)","swarm (5-10)","nest (10-20)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature appears to be a segmented worm or caterpillar with stumpy, almost nonexistent legs. Four tentacles sprout below its mouth.  The creature's body is covered in an oily film that leaves a slime path behind it as it moves.","group":"Slime Crawler","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Slippery","kind":"Ex","details":"A slime crawler exudes a thin, oily film from the glands beneath its mouth that leaves a slug-like trail behind it as its moves and provides the creature with a +6 racial bonus to Escape Artist checks. A creature stepping in a space covered with this slime must succeed on a DC 11 Reflex save or slip and fall prone. The slime remains in the area for 1d2 hours before losing its potency. The save DC is Constitution-based."}],"description":"Larval slime crawlers usually mature into this form within two to three weeks of hatching, at which time they feed on any living organisms encountered. More slug-like at this larval stage, the legs appear as small buds or stumps. These legs allow the slime crawler to climb walls and other surfaces, albeit slower than a mature slime crawler. Four tentacles sprout below its throat, eventually losing their grappling ability and growing into the pseudopod-like tentacles of the mature slime crawler.\n\nA typical larval slime crawler is about 6 ft. long and weighs about 300 pounds.\n\nA larval slime crawler attacks with its tentacles, attempting to grab an opponent and squeeze it. Slime crawlers have a nasty bite, but prefer to use their tentacles in battle.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Mandragora","cr":1,"xp":400,"alignment":"NE","size":"Small","type":"plant","subtypes":["fungus"],"init":1,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"tremorsense","modifier":"120 ft."}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":15,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +4 natural, +1 size","hp":5,"hd":"1d8+1","hdvalue":1,"fort":3,"ref":1,"will":0,"immunities":["plant traits"],"resists":[{"type":"fire","value":10}],"sr":12,"weaknesses":["light blindness"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"burrow","speed":20}],"melee":"2 tentacle slaps +2 (1d4 plus grab)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"constrict","details":"2d4"}],"strength":11,"dexterity":13,"constitution":13,"intelligence":8,"wisdom":10,"charisma":9,"baseatk":0,"cmb":-1,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":10,"feats":[{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse","type":"bonus"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":7},{"skill":"Stealth","value":5}],"environments":["temperate forests"],"organizations":["solitary","gang (2-5)","colony (3-18)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This small vaguely humanoid looking plant creature is mottled green and brown. Its lower roots are splayed and resemble legs and ft. Its upper roots are long and resemble humanoid arms. Its head, if it could be called that, is a mass of solid vegetable matter covered in lumps.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","description":"The mandragora is a small carnivorous and vaguely humanoid-looking plant that loves the taste of humanoid flesh (particularly human flesh).\n\nIf the mandragora goes more than three days without devouring flesh, it burrows into the ground and attaches to local tree roots, from which it draws its sustenance until living prey can be found.\n\nThe mandragora stands about 5 ft. tall and weighs about 120 pounds.\n\nA mandragora attacks its opponent by slashing and striking with its upper root-tentacles. It attempts to grab its prey and strangle it. If it succeeds, the mandragora hangs on until either it or its opponent is dead. Slain creatures are covered in a thick layer of mucus and slime and devoured at the mandragora's leisure.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Mandragora originally appeared in the First Edition Monster Manual II (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1983) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Margay","cr":1,"xp":400,"alignment":"N","size":"Small","type":"animal","init":3,"senses":[{"type":"superior low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":14,"ac_touch":14,"ac_flat_footed":11,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex, +1 size","hp":13,"hd":"2d8+4","hdvalue":2,"fort":5,"ref":6,"will":1,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":40},{"type":"climb","speed":30}],"melee":"bite +5 (1d4+1 plus grab), 2 claws +5 (1d3+1)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"pounce"},{"type":"rake","details":"2 claws +5, 1d3+1"}],"strength":13,"dexterity":17,"constitution":15,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":12,"charisma":6,"baseatk":1,"cmb":1,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":14,"cmd_special":"+4 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":11},{"skill":"Climb","value":15},{"skill":"Perception","value":10},{"skill":"Stealth","value":11}],"environments":["temperate forests","warm forests"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","family (3-5)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This jungle denizen appears as a large wild housecat with markings similar to a tiger.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Superior Low-Light Vision","kind":"Ex","details":"A margay can see three times as far as a human in starlight, moonlight, torchlight, and similar conditions of shadowy illumination. It retains the ability to distinguish color and detail under these conditions."}],"description":"Margays are jungle-dwelling cats similar in makeup and build to the ocelot. They favor dense forests with thick foliage and spend their days basking in the sun or hunting for food.\n\nA margay's diet includes small mammals, birds, bird eggs, small reptiles and amphibians, and fruits and nuts. They are active during both the night and day, having no set hunting or feeding time.\n\nMargays generally mate early in their life (usually within the first year) and gestation lasts about 80 days. Young are born live and a litter consists of one or two young. Young margays are dependent on their mother for about 8 weeks before being able to forage and hunt on their own.\n\nA margay looks like a small, slender, spotted cat. Its fur ranges in color from tan to brown or cinnamon. Its spots are darker and run in longitudinal rows along its body. Margays generally average about two ft. long and weigh about eight pounds.\n\nMargays attack with stealth and surprise but avoid conflict with creatures much larger than themselves unless cornered. When engaged, they attempt to pounce upon their prey and wrap their claws around the victims head, sinking their needle-like teeth into its throat.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Medium Barracuda","cr":1,"xp":400,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"animal","subtypes":["aquatic"],"init":2,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":13,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":11,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +1 natural","hp":16,"hd":"3d8+3","hdvalue":3,"fort":4,"ref":5,"will":2,"speed":[{"type":"swim","speed":60}],"melee":"bite +4 (1d6+1/19-20)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":12,"dexterity":14,"constitution":13,"intelligence":1,"wisdom":12,"charisma":2,"baseatk":2,"cmb":3,"cmd":15,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Improved Critical (bite)"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse","type":"bonus"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":9},{"skill":"Swim","value":14}],"environments":["any aquatic"],"organizations":["pair","pack (2-5)","school (6-11)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This fish is long and bluish gray fading to mottled silver near it tail. It has an oversized jaw full of sharp teeth.","group":"Barracuda","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","description":"The barracuda is a predatory fish that averages about two ft. long and weighs 10-15 pounds. Its body is bluish-gray near the front changing to silver mottled with black spots in the rear. Its mouth is filled with razor-sharp teeth. Medium barracudas are about six ft. long and weigh 30-45 pounds. Large barracudas are about ten ft. long and weigh 50-80 pounds.\n\nThe barracuda strikes quickly in combat. Slain prey is devoured immediately.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Medium Monstrous Sea Wasp","cr":1,"xp":400,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"vermin","subtypes":["aquatic"],"init":1,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"watersense","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":12,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":11,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +1 natural","hp":5,"hd":"1d8+1","hdvalue":1,"fort":3,"ref":1,"will":0,"defensive_abilities":["transparent"],"immunities":["vermin traits"],"speed":[{"type":"swim","speed":50}],"melee":"tentacle +1 (1d6-3 plus poison)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"entangle"}],"strength":7,"dexterity":13,"constitution":12,"wisdom":10,"charisma":2,"baseatk":0,"cmb":-3,"cmd":8,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Finesse","type":"bonus"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Escape Artist","value":11},{"skill":"Perception","value":4},{"skill":"Stealth","value":11},{"skill":"Swim","value":9}],"sq":[{"type":"jet"},{"type":"transparent"}],"environments":["warm aquatic"],"organizations":["solitary","ring (2-5)"],"treasure":"incidental","description_visual":"A translucent blue and nearly invisible in water, this cube-shaped jellyfish has four distinct sides and trails dozens of extremely long tentacles.","group":"Sea Wasp","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Entangle","kind":"Ex","details":"A sea wasp can entangle a creature up to two sizes smaller than itself with its tentacles with a successful CMB check. An entangled opponent suffers tentacle damage and the effect of its poison each round the hold is maintained. An entangled creature still receives a save to avoid the effects of its poison. While a foe is entangled, the sea wasp cannot attack any other creature."},{"type":"Poison","kind":"Ex","details":"Sting-injury; the effects of the poison are determined by the creature's size. The DC of the save is Constitution-based. Size Fort DC Frequency Effect Cure Tiny 10 1/round for 1d4 rounds 1 Con 1 save Small 11 1/round for 1d4 rounds 1d2 Con 1 save Medium 11 1/round for 1d6 rounds 1d3 Con 1 save Large 13 1/round for 1d6 rounds 1d4 Con 2 consecutive saves Huge 15 1/round for 1d6 rounds 1d6 Con 2 consecutive saves Gargantuan 20 1/round for 1d6 rounds 1d8 Con 3 consecutive saves"},{"type":"Jet","kind":"Ex","details":"A monstrous sea wasp can jet backward once per round as a full-round action at a speed equal to four times its swim speed. It must move in a straight line, but doesn't provoke attacks of opportunity while jetting."},{"type":"Transparent","kind":"Ex","details":"A monstrous sea wasp is nearly invisible floating in the water. An observer must make a successful DC 20 Perception check to notice the sea wasp."},{"type":"Watersense","kind":"Ex","details":"Monstrous sea wasps can automatically sense the location of anything within 60 ft. that is in contact with water."}],"description":"Monstrous sea wasps are deadly aquatic creatures with long, writhing and highly poisonous tentacles that extend for several yards. They inhabit warm waters and tend to congregate near coastal regions or river mouths where they wait for prey to wash downstream to them. Monstrous sea wasps are also called box jellyfish or marine stingers. Almost invisible in the water, the sea wasp has four eyes, one on each side of the bell, connected to a nerve ring inside the main body. They have no brain, but are able to process visual information. These eyes are invisible to all but the keenest observers. Monstrous sea wasps feed on a variety of marine life including fish, manta rays and stingrays, and crustaceans. The larger monstrous sea wasps also feed on sharks, whales, giant fish, giant squids, octopi, and swimmers. Prey that becomes entangled in its mass of tentacles is quickly stung by the sea wasp and then coiled into its mouth and devoured. Monstrous sea wasps are non-aggressive and don't actively hunt for food. They simply float along in the water waiting for a living creature to swim into its mass of tentacles. When it detects prey in its tentacles, it folds them around the prey, and stings it. A monstrous sea wasp's poison (particularly the larger ones) is meant to instantly kill its prey so it doesn't struggle and damage the sea wasp's delicate form. Sages catalogue the sea wasp's sting as being one of the deadliest and most painful one can experience. A creature stung by a sea wasp that lives to tell about, never forgets its brush with this monstrosity.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""}];
